    "qtype": "short_answer",
    "marks": 3,
    "text": "Prove that $\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta - \\cos\\theta + 1}{\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta - 1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta - \\tan\\theta}$.",
    "solution": "Divide the numerator and denominator of the LHS by $\\cos\\theta$: LHS $= \\dfrac{\\tan\\theta - 1 + \\sec\\theta}{\\tan\\theta + 1 - \\sec\\theta}$. Write $1 = \\sec^2\\theta - \\tan^2\\theta$ in the numerator: $\\tan\\theta + \\sec\\theta - (\\sec\\theta-\\tan\\theta)(\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta) = (\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta)(1 - \\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta)$. Cancelling the common factor $(\\tan\\theta + 1 - \\sec\\theta)$ gives LHS $= \\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta$. Now RHS $= \\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta - \\tan\\theta}\\times\\dfrac{\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta}{\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sec\\theta+\\tan\\theta}{\\sec^2\\theta - \\tan^2\\theta} = \\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta$. Hence LHS = RHS.",
    "marking_scheme": [
     {
      "step": "Divide the numerator and denominator by $\\cos\\theta$ to get $\\dfrac{\\tan\\theta + \\sec\\theta - 1}{\\tan\\theta - \\sec\\theta + 1}$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Replace 1 by $\\sec^2\\theta - \\tan^2\\theta$ in the numerator, factorise and cancel to get $\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Rationalise the RHS using $\\sec^2\\theta - \\tan^2\\theta = 1$ to get $\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta$, so LHS = RHS",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ],
