    "qtype": "long_answer",
    "marks": 5,
    "text": "If $\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta = p$, where $0^\\circ < \\theta < 90^\\circ$, show that $\\dfrac{p^2-1}{p^2+1} = \\sin\\theta$.",
    "answer": "Proved",
    "solution": "$p = \\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta = \\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$. Then $p^2 = \\frac{(1+\\sin\\theta)^2}{\\cos^2\\theta} = \\frac{(1+\\sin\\theta)^2}{1-\\sin^2\\theta} = \\frac{(1+\\sin\\theta)^2}{(1-\\sin\\theta)(1+\\sin\\theta)} = \\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}$. So $\\frac{p^2-1}{p^2+1} = \\frac{(1+\\sin\\theta)-(1-\\sin\\theta)}{(1+\\sin\\theta)+(1-\\sin\\theta)} = \\frac{2\\sin\\theta}{2} = \\sin\\theta$.",
    "marking_scheme": [
     {
      "step": "Writes $p = \\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}$ by converting $\\sec\\theta$ and $\\tan\\theta$ to sine and cosine",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Squares $p$ and replaces $\\cos^2\\theta$ by $1-\\sin^2\\theta$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Simplifies to $p^2 = \\frac{1+\\sin\\theta}{1-\\sin\\theta}$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Substitutes into $\\frac{p^2-1}{p^2+1}$ and simplifies numerator and denominator",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "step": "Obtains $\\sin\\theta$ and concludes",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ],
